    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old male named Thomas is diagnosed with orthostatic hypotension. Before initiating pharmacotherapy, his physician recommends non-pharmacological interventions. Which of the following is the most comprehensive and evidence-based set of non-pharmacological strategies?', '["Consuming a single large, high-carbohydrate meal per day to maintain plasma glucose levels and prevent hypotension", "Immediate bed rest for 2 weeks with complete avoidance of standing or walking to allow baroreceptor recovery", "Wearing loose-fitting, low-compression knee-high stockings and avoiding all physical activity", "Severe fluid restriction (< 1 L/day) and a strict low-sodium diet (< 2 g/day) to prevent fluid overload", "Adequate fluid intake (2-2.5 L/day unless contraindicated), liberal salt intake (6-10 g/day), waist-high compression stockings (30-40 mmHg) or abdominal binders, rise slowly from supine to sitting to standing, perform counter-pressure maneuvers (leg crossing, squatting), and avoid prolonged standing and hot environments"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Non-pharmacological management** is the foundation of OH treatment and should always be tried first: (1) **Increase fluid intake** to 2-2.5 L/day (bolus water drinking — 500 mL in 5 minutes — can raise SBP by 20-30 mmHg within 15-20 minutes via the osmopressor response), (2) **Liberal salt intake** (6-10 g/day, unless contraindicated by HF), (3) **Compression garments**: waist-high stockings (30-40 mmHg) or abdominal binders are more effective than knee-high stockings because they compress the splanchnic and pelvic venous reservoirs, (4) **Gradual postural changes**: sit on the edge of the bed before standing, (5) **Counter-pressure maneuvers**: leg crossing, toe raising, squatting, and isometric handgrip, (6) **Avoid triggers**: prolonged standing, hot environments, alcohol, large carbohydrate meals. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 55; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Freeman R, NEJM 2008)'),
