    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old male named Thomas is diagnosed with orthostatic hypotension. Before initiating pharmacotherapy, his physician recommends non-pharmacological interventions. Which of the following is the most comprehensive and evidence-based set of non-pharmacological strategies?', '["Wearing loose-fitting, low-compression knee-high stockings and avoiding all physical activity", "Immediate bed rest for 2 weeks with complete avoidance of standing or walking to allow baroreceptor recovery", "Consuming a single large, high-carbohydrate meal per day to maintain plasma glucose levels and prevent hypotension", "Adequate fluid intake (2-2.5 L/day unless contraindicated), liberal salt intake (6-10 g/day), waist-high compression stockings (30-40 mmHg) or abdominal binders, rise slowly from supine to sitting to standing, perform counter-pressure maneuvers (leg crossing, squatting), and avoid prolonged standing and hot environments", "Severe fluid restriction (< 1 L/day) and a strict low-sodium diet (< 2 g/day) to prevent fluid overload"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Non-pharmacological management** is the foundation of OH treatment and should always be tried first: (1) **Increase fluid intake** to 2-2.5 L/day (bolus water drinking — 500 mL in 5 minutes — can raise SBP by 20-30 mmHg within 15-20 minutes via the osmopressor response), (2) **Liberal salt intake** (6-10 g/day, unless contraindicated by HF), (3) **Compression garments**: waist-high stockings (30-40 mmHg) or abdominal binders are more effective than knee-high stockings because they compress the splanchnic and pelvic venous reservoirs, (4) **Gradual postural changes**: sit on the edge of the bed before standing, (5) **Counter-pressure maneuvers**: leg crossing, toe raising, squatting, and isometric handgrip, (6) **Avoid triggers**: prolonged standing, hot environments, alcohol, large carbohydrate meals. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 55; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Freeman R, NEJM 2008)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old female named Martha with severe symptomatic orthostatic hypotension (SBP drops 25 mmHg on standing) has failed non-pharmacological interventions. Her physician prescribes midodrine. What is the mechanism of action of midodrine, and what is the critical counseling point regarding its administration?', '["Midodrine is a beta-2 adrenergic agonist that increases heart rate and cardiac output; it should be taken only at bedtime", "Midodrine is a centrally acting alpha-2 agonist (like clonidine) that reduces sympathetic outflow; it has no timing restrictions", "Midodrine is a direct renin inhibitor that increases angiotensin II levels; it should be taken with a high-potassium meal", "Midodrine is an oral selective alpha-1 adrenergic agonist (prodrug converted to desglymidodrine) that causes peripheral arterial and venous vasoconstriction, raising standing blood pressure; the critical counseling point is that it must NOT be taken within 4 hours of bedtime because it causes dangerous supine hypertension", "Midodrine is a mineralocorticoid that expands plasma volume by promoting renal sodium retention; it should be taken with potassium supplements"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Midodrine** is an FDA-approved **oral selective alpha-1 adrenergic agonist** (prodrug; active metabolite = desglymidodrine) used for the treatment of symptomatic orthostatic hypotension. Mechanism: Activates **alpha-1 receptors** on peripheral **arterial and venous smooth muscle**, causing vasoconstriction that raises both standing and supine blood pressure. Typical dosing: 2.5-10 mg, 2-3 times daily **during waking/upright hours**. **Critical counseling**: (1) **Do NOT take within 4 hours of bedtime** — midodrine causes **supine hypertension**, which can lead to headache, blurred vision, and cardiovascular events during sleep, (2) Monitor supine BP at home, (3) Urinary retention is a contraindication. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 55; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; FDA Prescribing Information)'),
