    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old male named Herbert with neurogenic orthostatic hypotension secondary to diabetic autonomic neuropathy is started on fludrocortisone 0.1 mg daily. Which of the following best describes the mechanism of fludrocortisone and the primary monitoring concern in this elderly patient?', '["Fludrocortisone is an alpha-1 agonist that directly constricts peripheral blood vessels without any effect on renal sodium handling", "Fludrocortisone causes renal potassium retention and sodium wasting, requiring potassium restriction and sodium supplementation", "Fludrocortisone is a synthetic mineralocorticoid that promotes renal sodium and water reabsorption in the collecting duct (via epithelial sodium channels, ENaC), expanding plasma volume; the primary monitoring concerns are supine hypertension, hypokalemia, peripheral edema, and worsening of heart failure", "Fludrocortisone is a glucocorticoid that suppresses ACTH secretion and reduces cortisol levels, having no effect on blood pressure", "Fludrocortisone is a loop diuretic that reduces total blood volume to prevent venous pooling in the lower extremities"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Fludrocortisone** (Florinef) is a synthetic **mineralocorticoid** used off-label for orthostatic hypotension. Mechanism: It activates **mineralocorticoid receptors** in the renal collecting duct, upregulating the **epithelial sodium channel (ENaC)** and the **Na+/K+ ATPase**, causing: (1) **Increased sodium and water reabsorption** (expands plasma volume by 10-20%), (2) **Increased potassium excretion** (risk of hypokalemia). Additional mechanism: At higher doses, it sensitizes alpha-1 adrenergic receptors on blood vessels, enhancing the vasoconstrictive response. **Monitoring concerns in elderly**: (1) **Supine hypertension** (worsened by volume expansion), (2) **Hypokalemia**, (3) **Peripheral edema**, (4) **Heart failure exacerbation** (CONTRAINDICATED in patients with HF). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 55; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Freeman R, NEJM 2008)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old male named Herbert with Parkinson''s disease and refractory neurogenic orthostatic hypotension (SBP drops 25 mmHg on standing) has failed midodrine and non-pharmacological therapies. His neurologist prescribes droxidopa. What is the mechanism of action of droxidopa?', '["Droxidopa (L-threo-3,4-dihydroxyphenylserine) is a synthetic norepinephrine precursor (prodrug) that is converted to norepinephrine by aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (DOPA decarboxylase), both peripherally and centrally, directly replenishing the depleted norepinephrine stores in patients with autonomic failure", "Droxidopa is a competitive antagonist of acetylcholine at muscarinic M3 receptors, preventing parasympathetic-mediated vasodilation", "Droxidopa is a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI) that increases serotonin levels in the brainstem cardiovascular centers", "Droxidopa is a mineralocorticoid receptor agonist that promotes sodium retention in the renal collecting duct", "Droxidopa is a dopamine agonist (like pramipexole) that improves motor function in Parkinson''s disease and has no effect on blood pressure"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Droxidopa** (Northera) is FDA-approved for the treatment of **neurogenic orthostatic hypotension** (nOH), particularly in patients with **Parkinson''s disease, multiple system atrophy (MSA), and pure autonomic failure**. Mechanism: Droxidopa is a **synthetic amino acid precursor of norepinephrine**. It is converted to **norepinephrine** by the enzyme **aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (DOPA decarboxylase)**, bypassing the rate-limiting step of dopamine beta-hydroxylase. This directly **replenishes depleted norepinephrine stores** at peripheral sympathetic nerve terminals and in the CNS, restoring the sympathetic vasoconstrictive response to standing. Unlike midodrine (which acts on alpha-1 receptors), droxidopa provides the actual neurotransmitter. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 55; Kaufmann H et al., Neurology 2014; FDA Prescribing Information)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old male named Eugene with Parkinson''s disease and refractory neurogenic orthostatic hypotension (SBP drops 30 mmHg on standing) has failed midodrine and non-pharmacological therapies. His neurologist prescribes droxidopa. What is the mechanism of action of droxidopa?', '["Droxidopa is a mineralocorticoid receptor agonist that promotes sodium retention in the renal collecting duct", "Droxidopa is a competitive antagonist of acetylcholine at muscarinic M3 receptors, preventing parasympathetic-mediated vasodilation", "Droxidopa is a dopamine agonist (like pramipexole) that improves motor function in Parkinson''s disease and has no effect on blood pressure", "Droxidopa is a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI) that increases serotonin levels in the brainstem cardiovascular centers", "Droxidopa (L-threo-3,4-dihydroxyphenylserine) is a synthetic norepinephrine precursor (prodrug) that is converted to norepinephrine by aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (DOPA decarboxylase), both peripherally and centrally, directly replenishing the depleted norepinephrine stores in patients with autonomic failure"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Droxidopa** (Northera) is FDA-approved for the treatment of **neurogenic orthostatic hypotension** (nOH), particularly in patients with **Parkinson''s disease, multiple system atrophy (MSA), and pure autonomic failure**. Mechanism: Droxidopa is a **synthetic amino acid precursor of norepinephrine**. It is converted to **norepinephrine** by the enzyme **aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (DOPA decarboxylase)**, bypassing the rate-limiting step of dopamine beta-hydroxylase. This directly **replenishes depleted norepinephrine stores** at peripheral sympathetic nerve terminals and in the CNS, restoring the sympathetic vasoconstrictive response to standing. Unlike midodrine (which acts on alpha-1 receptors), droxidopa provides the actual neurotransmitter. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 55; Kaufmann H et al., Neurology 2014; FDA Prescribing Information)'),
