    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old female named Ruth with Parkinson''s disease and refractory neurogenic orthostatic hypotension (SBP drops 25 mmHg on standing) has failed midodrine and non-pharmacological therapies. Her neurologist prescribes droxidopa. What is the mechanism of action of droxidopa?', '["Droxidopa (L-threo-3,4-dihydroxyphenylserine) is a synthetic norepinephrine precursor (prodrug) that is converted to norepinephrine by aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (DOPA decarboxylase), both peripherally and centrally, directly replenishing the depleted norepinephrine stores in patients with autonomic failure", "Droxidopa is a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI) that increases serotonin levels in the brainstem cardiovascular centers", "Droxidopa is a dopamine agonist (like pramipexole) that improves motor function in Parkinson''s disease and has no effect on blood pressure", "Droxidopa is a mineralocorticoid receptor agonist that promotes sodium retention in the renal collecting duct", "Droxidopa is a competitive antagonist of acetylcholine at muscarinic M3 receptors, preventing parasympathetic-mediated vasodilation"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Droxidopa** (Northera) is FDA-approved for the treatment of **neurogenic orthostatic hypotension** (nOH), particularly in patients with **Parkinson''s disease, multiple system atrophy (MSA), and pure autonomic failure**. Mechanism: Droxidopa is a **synthetic amino acid precursor of norepinephrine**. It is converted to **norepinephrine** by the enzyme **aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase (DOPA decarboxylase)**, bypassing the rate-limiting step of dopamine beta-hydroxylase. This directly **replenishes depleted norepinephrine stores** at peripheral sympathetic nerve terminals and in the CNS, restoring the sympathetic vasoconstrictive response to standing. Unlike midodrine (which acts on alpha-1 receptors), droxidopa provides the actual neurotransmitter. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 55; Kaufmann H et al., Neurology 2014; FDA Prescribing Information)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old male named Clarence presents after a mechanical fall at home. He tripped on a rug while walking to the bathroom at night. As part of the comprehensive geriatric falls assessment, when should orthostatic blood pressure be routinely measured?', '["Orthostatic blood pressure is only relevant in hospitalized patients and should never be measured in the outpatient setting", "Orthostatic blood pressure should only be measured in patients with a confirmed diagnosis of Parkinson''s disease or multiple system atrophy", "Orthostatic blood pressure should only be measured if the patient explicitly reports positional dizziness; falls alone are not an indication", "Orthostatic blood pressure testing is obsolete and has been replaced entirely by 24-hour ambulatory blood pressure monitoring", "Orthostatic blood pressure measurement should be performed in ALL older adults who present with falls, syncope, presyncope, or dizziness, and routinely at health assessments in those aged >= 65; it should also be checked after starting or adjusting any antihypertensive, diuretic, vasodilator, or psychotropic medication"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Orthostatic BP measurement is a critical component of the comprehensive geriatric falls assessment** and should be performed in: (1) **ALL older adults presenting with falls, syncope, presyncope, lightheadedness, or unexplained weakness**, (2) **Routine health assessments in adults >= 65**, (3) **After initiation or dose adjustment** of any medication that can cause OH (antihypertensives, diuretics, alpha-blockers, nitrates, tricyclic antidepressants, antipsychotics, opioids, dopaminergic agents), (4) **Hospital admission and discharge** assessments. Technique: Supine BP after 5 minutes rest, then standing BP at 1 minute and 3 minutes. Note that OH can be **asymptomatic** in up to 50% of affected older adults (especially those with cognitive impairment). (Ref: AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines 2022; Hazzard''s Ch. 55; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
