    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old male named Richard presents after a mechanical fall at home. He tripped on a rug while walking to the bathroom at night. As part of the comprehensive geriatric falls assessment, when should orthostatic blood pressure be routinely measured?', '["Orthostatic blood pressure testing is obsolete and has been replaced entirely by 24-hour ambulatory blood pressure monitoring", "Orthostatic blood pressure measurement should be performed in ALL older adults who present with falls, syncope, presyncope, or dizziness, and routinely at health assessments in those aged >= 65; it should also be checked after starting or adjusting any antihypertensive, diuretic, vasodilator, or psychotropic medication", "Orthostatic blood pressure should only be measured if the patient explicitly reports positional dizziness; falls alone are not an indication", "Orthostatic blood pressure should only be measured in patients with a confirmed diagnosis of Parkinson''s disease or multiple system atrophy", "Orthostatic blood pressure is only relevant in hospitalized patients and should never be measured in the outpatient setting"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Orthostatic BP measurement is a critical component of the comprehensive geriatric falls assessment** and should be performed in: (1) **ALL older adults presenting with falls, syncope, presyncope, lightheadedness, or unexplained weakness**, (2) **Routine health assessments in adults >= 65**, (3) **After initiation or dose adjustment** of any medication that can cause OH (antihypertensives, diuretics, alpha-blockers, nitrates, tricyclic antidepressants, antipsychotics, opioids, dopaminergic agents), (4) **Hospital admission and discharge** assessments. Technique: Supine BP after 5 minutes rest, then standing BP at 1 minute and 3 minutes. Note that OH can be **asymptomatic** in up to 50% of affected older adults (especially those with cognitive impairment). (Ref: AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines 2022; Hazzard''s Ch. 55; AGS Geriat