    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old female named Irene presents after a mechanical fall at home. She tripped on a rug while walking to the bathroom at night. As part of the comprehensive geriatric falls assessment, when should orthostatic blood pressure be routinely measured?', '["Orthostatic blood pressure testing is obsolete and has been replaced entirely by 24-hour ambulatory blood pressure monitoring", "Orthostatic blood pressure should only be measured if the patient explicitly reports positional dizziness; falls alone are not an indication", "Orthostatic blood pressure measurement should be performed in ALL older adults who present with falls, syncope, presyncope, or dizziness, and routinely at health assessments in those aged >= 65; it should also be checked after starting or adjusting any antihypertensive, diuretic, vasodilator, or psychotropic medication", "Orthostatic blood pressure is only relevant in hospitalized patients and should never be measured in the outpatient setting", "Orthostatic blood pressure should only be measured in patients with a confirmed diagnosis of Parkinson''s disease or multiple system atrophy"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Orthostatic BP measurement is a critical component of the comprehensive geriatric falls assessment** and should be performed in: (1) **ALL older adults presenting with falls, syncope, presyncope, lightheadedness, or unexplained weakness**, (2) **Routine health assessments in adults >= 65**, (3) **After initiation or dose adjustment** of any medication that can cause OH (antihypertensives, diuretics, alpha-blockers, nitrates, tricyclic antidepressants, antipsychotics, opioids, dopaminergic agents), (4) **Hospital admission and discharge** assessments. Technique: Supine BP after 5 minutes rest, then standing BP at 1 minute and 3 minutes. Note that OH can be **asymptomatic** in up to 50% of affected older adults (especially those with cognitive impairment). (Ref: AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines 2022; Hazzard''s Ch. 55; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old female named Florence presents after a mechanical fall at home. She tripped on a rug while walking to the bathroom at night. As part of the comprehensive geriatric falls assessment, when should orthostatic blood pressure be routinely measured?', '["Orthostatic blood pressure measurement should be performed in ALL older adults who present with falls, syncope, presyncope, or dizziness, and routinely at health assessments in those aged >= 65; it should also be checked after starting or adjusting any antihypertensive, diuretic, vasodilator, or psychotropic medication", "Orthostatic blood pressure should only be measured if the patient explicitly reports positional dizziness; falls alone are not an indication", "Orthostatic blood pressure is only relevant in hospitalized patients and should never be measured in the outpatient setting", "Orthostatic blood pressure testing is obsolete and has been replaced entirely by 24-hour ambulatory blood pressure monitoring", "Orthostatic blood pressure should only be measured in patients with a confirmed diagnosis of Parkinson''s disease or multiple system atrophy"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Orthostatic BP measurement is a critical component of the comprehensive geriatric falls assessment** and should be performed in: (1) **ALL older adults presenting with falls, syncope, presyncope, lightheadedness, or unexplained weakness**, (2) **Routine health assessments in adults >= 65**, (3) **After initiation or dose adjustment** of any medication that can cause OH (antihypertensives, diuretics, alpha-blockers, nitrates, tricyclic antidepressants, antipsychotics, opioids, dopaminergic agents), (4) **Hospital admission and discharge** assessments. Technique: Supine BP after 5 minutes rest, then standing BP at 1 minute and 3 minutes. Note that OH can be **asymptomatic** in up to 50% of affected older adults (especially those with cognitive impairment). (Ref: AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines 2022; Hazzard''s Ch. 55; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-year-old male named Edward has recurrent unexplained syncope. Bedside orthostatic blood pressure measurements have been inconclusive. Which of the following is the most appropriate next diagnostic test to evaluate for orthostatic or vasovagal mechanisms of syncope?', '["Carotid duplex ultrasound to evaluate for carotid artery stenosis as the primary cause of orthostatic syncope", "Coronary angiography to evaluate for critical coronary artery stenosis as the cause of recurrent syncope", "24-hour Holter monitor as the definitive test for all forms of syncope including orthostatic hypotension", "Head-up tilt-table testing; the patient is tilted to 60-70 degrees for up to 45 minutes while continuous BP and heart rate are monitored, with optional isoproterenol or nitroglycerin provocation to unmask vasovagal or orthostatic mechanisms", "Lumbar puncture to measure cerebrospinal fluid pressure and rule out normal pressure hydrocephalus as the cause of syncope"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Head-up tilt-table testing** is the gold standard provocative test for evaluating **unexplained recurrent syncope** when bedside orthostatic measurements are inconclusive. Protocol: (1) Patient lies supine for 20 minutes on a motorized table, (2) Table is tilted to **60-70 degrees** (simulating standing), (3) Continuous beat-to-beat BP and heart rate are monitored for up to **45 minutes**, (4) Optional pharmacological provocation with sublingual nitroglycerin or IV isoproterenol. Positive results: (1) **Vasovagal syncope (neurocardiogenic)**: sudden bradycardia and/or hypotension with reproduction of symptoms, (2) **Orthostatic hypotension**: sustained progressive BP drop meeting criteria, (3) **POTS (postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome)**: HR increase >= 30 bpm without significant BP drop. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 55; ESC Syncope Guidelines 2018; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
