    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old male named Arthur has recurrent unexplained syncope. Bedside orthostatic blood pressure measurements have been inconclusive. Which of the following is the most appropriate next diagnostic test to evaluate for orthostatic or vasovagal mechanisms of syncope?', '["Carotid duplex ultrasound to evaluate for carotid artery stenosis as the primary cause of orthostatic syncope", "Coronary angiography to evaluate for critical coronary artery stenosis as the cause of recurrent syncope", "24-hour Holter monitor as the definitive test for all forms of syncope including orthostatic hypotension", "Lumbar puncture to measure cerebrospinal fluid pressure and rule out normal pressure hydrocephalus as the cause of syncope", "Head-up tilt-table testing; the patient is tilted to 60-70 degrees for up to 45 minutes while continuous BP and heart rate are monitored, with optional isoproterenol or nitroglycerin provocation to unmask vasovagal or orthostatic mechanisms"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Head-up tilt-table testing** is the gold standard provocative test for evaluating **unexplained recurrent syncope** when bedside orthostatic measurements are inconclusive. Protocol: (1) Patient lies supine for 20 minutes on a motorized table, (2) Table is tilted to **60-70 degrees** (simulating standing), (3) Continuous beat-to-beat BP and heart rate are monitored for up to **45 minutes**, (4) Optional pharmacological provocation with sublingual nitroglycerin or IV isoproterenol. Positive results: (1) **Vasovagal syncope (neurocardiogenic)**: sudden bradycardia and/or hypotension with reproduction of symptoms, (2) **Orthostatic hypotension**: sustained progressive BP drop meeting criteria, (3) **POTS (postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome)**: HR increase >= 30 bpm without significant BP drop. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 55; ESC Syncope Guidelines 2018; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old female named Frances with a 8-year history of poorly controlled type 2 diabetes mellitus (HbA1c 9.2%) presents with chronic orthostatic hypotension, gastroparesis, and erectile dysfunction (if male) / urinary retention (if female). What is the unifying diagnosis, and what is the underlying mechanism?', '["Vitamin B12 deficiency; subacute combined degeneration of the dorsal columns and corticospinal tracts", "Diabetic autonomic neuropathy; chronic hyperglycemia causes oxidative stress, accumulation of advanced glycation end-products (AGEs), and polyol pathway activation, leading to progressive degeneration of small unmyelinated autonomic nerve fibers (C-fibers) in the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems", "Hypothyroidism; thyroid hormone deficiency causing global autonomic dysfunction and requires levothyroxine replacement", "Diabetic ketoacidosis; acute insulin deficiency causing osmotic dehydration, which resolves completely with fluid resuscitation", "Pheochromocytoma; episodic catecholamine surges causing paroxysmal hypertension alternating with orthostatic hypotension"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Diabetic autonomic neuropathy (DAN)** is a common and underdiagnosed complication of long-standing, poorly controlled diabetes. It affects the **small unmyelinated C-fibers** of both the **sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems**. Pathophysiology: (1) **Oxidative stress** from chronic hyperglycemia, (2) **Advanced glycation end-products (AGEs)** damaging nerve fibers and vasa nervorum, (3) **Polyol pathway activation** (sorbitol accumulation causing osmotic nerve injury). Clinical manifestations include: **Cardiovascular** (orthostatic hypotension, resting tachycardia, exercise intolerance), **Gastrointestinal** (gastroparesis, constipation, diarrhea), **Genitourinary** (neurogenic bladder, erectile dysfunction), **Sudomotor** (anhidrosis of feet, gustatory sweating). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 55; ADA Standards of Care 2024; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-year-old female named Lillian with a 8-year history of poorly controlled type 2 diabetes mellitus (HbA1c 9.2%) presents with chronic orthostatic hypotension, gastroparesis, and erectile dysfunction (if male) / urinary retention (if female). What is the unifying diagnosis, and what is the underlying mechanism?', '["Diabetic ketoacidosis; acute insulin deficiency causing osmotic dehydration, which resolves completely with fluid resuscitation", "Pheochromocytoma; episodic catecholamine surges causing paroxysmal hypertension alternating with orthostatic hypotension", "Hypothyroidism; thyroid hormone deficiency causing global autonomic dysfunction and requires levothyroxine replacement", "Vitamin B12 deficiency; subacute combined degeneration of the dorsal columns and corticospinal tracts", "Diabetic autonomic neuropathy; chronic hyperglycemia causes oxidative stress, accumulation of advanced glycation end-products (AGEs), and polyol pathway activation, leading to progressive degeneration of small unmyelinated autonomic nerve fibers (C-fibers) in the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Diabetic autonomic neuropathy (DAN)** is a common and underdiagnosed complication of long-standing, poorly controlled diabetes. It affects the **small unmyelinated C-fibers** of both the **sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems**. Pathophysiology: (1) **Oxidative stress** from chronic hyperglycemia, (2) **Advanced glycation end-products (AGEs)** damaging nerve fibers and vasa nervorum, (3) **Polyol pathway activation** (sorbitol accumulation causing osmotic nerve injury). Clinical manifestations include: **Cardiovascular** (orthostatic hypotension, resting tachycardia, exercise intolerance), **Gastrointestinal** (gastroparesis, constipation, diarrhea), **Genitourinary** (neurogenic bladder, erectile dysfunction), **Sudomotor** (anhidrosis of feet, gustatory sweating). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 55; ADA Standards of Care 2024; UWorld St