2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 96-year-old male named Roy presents with progressive exertional dyspnea, bilateral lower-extremity edema, and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea over the past 4 months. Echocardiography reveals a left ventricular ejection fraction (LVEF) of 65%, increased left ventricular wall thickness (concentric hypertrophy), elevated E/e'' ratio of 18, and enlarged left atrial volume index of 40 mL/m2. NT-proBNP is 1400 pg/mL. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Acute pulmonary embolism causing right heart strain with preserved left ventricular function", "Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF); defined as clinical heart failure symptoms and signs with LVEF >= 50%, evidence of diastolic dysfunction (elevated E/e'' ratio, enlarged left atrium), and elevated natriuretic peptides (BNP or NT-proBNP)", "Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF); defined as LVEF <= 40% with systolic pump failure", "Constrictive pericarditis causing equalization of diastolic pressures in all four cardiac chambers", "Restrictive cardiomyopathy caused exclusively by cardiac amyloidosis with no diastolic dysfunction"]'::jsonb, 1, '**HFpEF (Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction)** accounts for approximately **50% of all heart failure** and is the **predominant form in older adults**. Diagnostic criteria (2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guidelines): (1) **Clinical symptoms and signs** of heart failure (dyspnea, edema, orthopnea, PND), (2) **LVEF >= 50%** on echocardiography, (3) **Objective evidence of diastolic dysfunction**: elevated **E/e'' ratio** (>= 14 suggests elevated LV filling pressures), enlarged **left atrial volume index** (> 34 mL/m2), and (4) **Elevated natriuretic peptides** (BNP > 35 pg/mL or NT-proBNP > 125 pg/mL). HFpEF is distinct from HFrEF (LVEF <= 40%), which represents systolic pump failure. (Ref: 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA HF Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 38; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
