    (v_topic_id, 'A 96-year-old male named George presents with progressive exertional dyspnea, bilateral lower-extremity edema, and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea over the past 8 months. Echocardiography reveals a left ventricular ejection fraction (LVEF) of 68%, increased left ventricular wall thickness (concentric hypertrophy), elevated E/e'' ratio of 16, and enlarged left atrial volume index of 42 mL/m2. NT-proBNP is 780 pg/mL. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Constrictive pericarditis causing equalization of diastolic pressures in all four cardiac chambers", "Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF); defined as LVEF <= 40% with systolic pump failure", "Restrictive cardiomyopathy caused exclusively by cardiac amyloidosis with no diastolic dysfunction", "Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF); defined as clinical heart failure symptoms and signs with LVEF >= 50%, evidence of diastolic dysfunction (elevated E/e'' ratio, enlarged left atrium), and elevated natriuretic peptides (BNP or NT-proBNP)", "Acute pulmonary embolism causing right heart strain with preserved left ventricular function"]'::jsonb, 3, '**HFpEF (Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction)** accounts for approximately **50% of all heart failure** and is the **predominant form in older adults**. Diagnostic criteria (2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guidelines): (1) **Clinical symptoms and signs** of heart failure (dyspnea, edema, orthopnea, PND), (2) **LVEF >= 50%** on echocardiography, (3) **Objective evidence of diastolic dysfunction**: elevated **E/e'' ratio** (>= 14 suggests elevated LV filling pressures), enlarged **left atrial volume index** (> 34 mL/m2), and (4) **Elevated natriuretic peptides** (BNP > 35 pg/mL or NT-proBNP > 125 pg/mL). HFpEF is distinct from HFrEF (LVEF <= 40%), which represents systolic pump failure. (Ref: 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA HF Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 38; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old male named Henry is diagnosed with HFpEF. His echocardiogram shows concentric left ventricular hypertrophy with preserved systolic function but impaired relaxation. What is the primary pathophysiological mechanism underlying HFpEF?', '["Cardiac tamponade from pericardial effusion compressing all four cardiac chambers equally", "Severe systolic contractile failure with a markedly dilated, thin-walled left ventricle and global hypokinesis", "Impaired left ventricular diastolic relaxation and increased passive myocardial stiffness (due to LV hypertrophy, myocardial fibrosis, and titin isoform changes), leading to elevated LV filling pressures, left atrial hypertension, pulmonary congestion, and exercise intolerance, despite normal systolic ejection", "Right ventricular failure due to chronic pulmonary embolism (cor pulmonale) with no involvement of the left ventricle", "Primary mitral valve regurgitation causing volume overload and eccentric left ventricular dilation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The hallmark pathophysiology of **HFpEF** is **diastolic dysfunction** — the left ventricle contracts normally (preserved EF) but **cannot relax and fill adequately during diastole**. Key mechanisms: (1) **Impaired active relaxation**: the myocardium fails to relax normally during early diastole (due to abnormal calcium reuptake by SERCA2a), (2) **Increased passive stiffness**: due to **concentric LV hypertrophy** (from chronic HTN), **myocardial interstitial fibrosis** (collagen deposition), and **titin isoform switching** (from the compliant N2BA to the stiff N2B isoform). Consequences: (3) Elevated LV end-diastolic pressure → (4) Elevated left atrial pressure → (5) **Pulmonary venous congestion** (dyspnea, orthopnea) and (6) Reduced cardiac output during exercise (exercise intolerance). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 38; Borlaug BA, JACC 2014; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
