    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old male named Donald with a BMI of 30 kg/m2, long-standing hypertension, type 2 diabetes mellitus, and atrial fibrillation is diagnosed with HFpEF. Which of the following statements most accurately describes the epidemiology and risk factor profile of HFpEF?', '["HFpEF has no known risk factors and occurs randomly in the general population without any demographic predilection", "HFpEF exclusively affects young, athletic males under 40 and is caused solely by genetic cardiomyopathy mutations", "HFpEF only occurs in patients who have previously had HFrEF and have recovered their ejection fraction after treatment", "HFpEF predominantly affects older women and is strongly associated with hypertension, obesity, diabetes mellitus, atrial fibrillation, and chronic kidney disease; it accounts for approximately 50% of all heart failure hospitalizations and carries a 5-year mortality rate of 50-75%", "HFpEF is a benign condition with normal life expectancy and no associated morbidity if ejection fraction is preserved"]'::jsonb, 3, '**HFpEF epidemiology** reveals a distinctive patient profile: (1) **Older age** (mean age at diagnosis > 70 years), (2) **Female predominance** (60-70%), (3) **Obesity** (BMI > 30 in > 80%), (4) **Hypertension** (most common comorbidity, present in > 80%), (5) **Type 2 diabetes mellitus** (40-50%), (6) **Atrial fibrillation** (50-65%), (7) **Chronic kidney disease**. HFpEF accounts for approximately **50% of all HF hospitalizations** and carries a substantial **5-year mortality rate of 50-75%**, comparable to HFrEF. Unlike HFrEF, where neurohormonal therapies (ACEi, BB, MRA) dramatically improve survival, until recently there were **no proven mortality-reducing therapies** for HFpEF. (Ref: 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA HF Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 38; Dunlay SM et al., Nat Rev Cardiol 2017)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old male named Joseph with a BMI of 32 kg/m2, long-standing hypertension, type 2 diabetes mellitus, and atrial fibrillation is diagnosed with HFpEF. Which of the following statements most accurately describes the epidemiology and risk factor profile of HFpEF?', '["HFpEF has no known risk factors and occurs randomly in the general population without any demographic predilection", "HFpEF exclusively affects young, athletic males under 40 and is caused solely by genetic cardiomyopathy mutations", "HFpEF is a benign condition with normal life expectancy and no associated morbidity if ejection fraction is preserved", "HFpEF only occurs in patients who have previously had HFrEF and have recovered their ejection fraction after treatment", "HFpEF predominantly affects older women and is strongly associated with hypertension, obesity, diabetes mellitus, atrial fibrillation, and chronic kidney disease; it accounts for approximately 50% of all heart failure hospitalizations and carries a 5-year mortality rate of 50-75%"]'::jsonb, 4, '**HFpEF epidemiology** reveals a distinctive patient profile: (1) **Older age** (mean age at diagnosis > 70 years), (2) **Female predominance** (60-70%), (3) **Obesity** (BMI > 30 in > 80%), (4) **Hypertension** (most common comorbidity, present in > 80%), (5) **Type 2 diabetes mellitus** (40-50%), (6) **Atrial fibrillation** (50-65%), (7) **Chronic kidney disease**. HFpEF accounts for approximately **50% of all HF hospitalizations** and carries a substantial **5-year mortality rate of 50-75%**, comparable to HFrEF. Unlike HFrEF, where neurohormonal therapies (ACEi, BB, MRA) dramatically improve survival, until recently there were **no proven mortality-reducing therapies** for HFpEF. (Ref: 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA HF Guidelines; Hazzard''s Ch. 38; Dunlay SM et al., Nat Rev Cardiol 2017)'),
